## Custom Audit Rules for nginx2root IOC Detection

## NGINX has been found to have multiple critical vulnerabilities that allow unauthenticated remote code execution.
## These vulnerabilities can be exploited to gain control of the NGINX worker processes, leading to potential system compromise,
## and subsequent privilege escalation to root through a related CVE.

## ref: https://xint.io/blog/nginx2root-remote-code-execution

## NGINX Vulnerabilities:
## CVE-2026-123007
## CVE-2026-123634
## CVE-2026-123633
## CVE-2026-121810
## CVE-2026-113425

## NGINX workers should not execute external programs under normal conditions. Any execution by the worker process
## could indicate an attack. The following steps use Auditd to monitor for unauthorized command execution by NGINX workers.

## Add to /etc/audit/rules.d/52-nginx2root.rules and reload with 'auditctl -R' or reboot. Check the new rule has been loaded.

## Step 1: Identify the NGINX Worker UID
## First, determine the UID running the NGINX worker processes:

## ps -eo uid,user,pid,ppid,args | grep 'nginx: worker process'

## Step 2: Configure Auditd Rules
## Create an Auditd rule to monitor for execve and execveat calls made by the NGINX worker UID:
## Replace NGINX_UID with the numeric UID from the previous output and add the following rule

-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve,execveat -F euid=NGINX_UID -k nginx_worker_exec

## Step 3: Review Audit Logs
## NB: The rule records executions only after it is installed. An empty result cannot rule out an earlier compromise.
## It also matches other processes using the same UID, so check the process and parent process IDs to establish whether an event came from nginx.

## Search records in the SIEM, but if only local logs are available, review the records with:
## sudo ausearch -k nginx_worker_exec -ts recent -i

## Look for entries where /bin/sh, /bin/bash, or other external programs are executed. Verify the parent process IDs to ensure the activity is related to NGINX.
